<!-- ── Static description (indexed by Google) ─────────────────────────────── -->
<p class="race-desc">
	Last Soul Ultra 2026 ran from August 14 to August 18 in the forest at Fürstenau, Germany. One hundred and sixty runners set off at 2 pm on the Friday, and every hour on the hour they went round again on a 6.7 km loop. Four days later Mark Dowdle was the only one left, having covered 94 loops and 629.8 km. Ayyoub "Ello" El Osrouti was the assist. Below are the lap splits for every runner who started, and a breakdown of where the field came apart.
</p>

{% include "race-analysis-charts.njk" %}

<!-- ── How it unfolded ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="page-wrap page-wrap--no-bottom">
	<div class="page-section">
		<h2>The first night took half the race</h2>
		<p>
			One hundred and sixty runners started. Eighty-two of them were gone before Saturday lunchtime. The damage was concentrated in a few dark hours: eighteen runners finished loop 15 at four in the morning and did not go back out, and ten more stopped an hour later. By six on Saturday morning the field had fallen from 123 to 78.
		</p>
		<p>
			The middle of the race thinned it further without ever being dramatic. Fifty-seven runners were still going after 24 loops. Thirteen made it past 48. Only three made it past 72. The median run was 16 loops, which is to say that half the field never saw a second sunrise.
		</p>

		<h2>Two men and thirteen hours</h2>
		<p>
			Norman Mascher-Aspensjö held third place deep into Monday and stopped after 80 loops at nine in the evening. That left Dowdle and El Osrouti alone on the loop, and they stayed that way through the whole of Monday night and on into Tuesday morning, thirteen hours of two men circling a forest with nobody else on the course.
		</p>
		<p>
			The numbers say El Osrouti was the faster runner for the entire race. He averaged 48:29 a loop against Dowdle's 51:42, and by getting round quicker he banked 5 more hours of rest than Dowdle over the four days.
		</p>
		<p>
			On loop 87, at four o'clock on Tuesday morning, on his fourth night without proper sleep, Mark Dowdle ran 38:15.</p>
		<p>It was the fastest loop of his race by four and a half minutes. His slowest loop of the whole four days was 58:03, back on loop 15 on the first night.
		</p>

		<h2>Familiar name at the front</h2>
		<p>
			Dowdle won <a href="/races/g1m-2026/">BPN Go One More in April</a> with 73 loops. He beats his own PB by 21 loops at the Last Soul Ultra just four months later.
		</p>
		<p>
			Behind the leaders, Caroline Maria Bolte ran 52 loops. She finished ninth overall, the best of the 42 women in the field, and set a new female German record.</p>
		<p>Hubert Karl, who is 68, ran 45, also setting a record in his age category for the most loops.</p>
		<p>Harvey Lewis, twice a Badwater champion, stopped at 46.</p>
	</p>
</div>

